.TH RECODEX-GUARDIAN 1 "2026" "recodex-guardian 0.1.0" "ReCodEx Guardian Manual"
.SH NAME
recodex-guardian, isolate \- Isolate-compatible sandbox for running untrusted code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isolate
.B \-\-init
.BI \-\-box\-id= ID
.br
.B isolate
.B \-\-run
.BI \-\-box\-id= ID
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I program
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B isolate
.B \-\-cleanup
.BI \-\-box\-id= ID
.br
.B isolate
.BI \-\-yaml= FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B recodex-guardian
runs a single program inside a restricted sandbox built from Linux namespaces,
cgroups v2, a dedicated UID/GID, and a private filesystem view, measuring its
resource usage. It is a drop-in replacement for the
.B isolate
sandbox used by the ReCodEx evaluator and is normally invoked through its
.B isolate
alias.
.PP
The tool has two interfaces. The
.B compatibility
interface mirrors isolate: three independent invocations \(em
.BR \-\-init ,
.BR \-\-run ,
.B \-\-cleanup
\(em keyed by a caller-supplied
.BR \-\-box\-id ,
sharing state only on disk, emitting an isolate-format meta-file and
0/1/2 exit codes. The
.B standalone
interface
.RB ( \-\-yaml )
runs a batch of tasks described by one YAML config and emits per-task
stats-yaml.
.PP
The binary is installed setuid root (mode 4755) so the unprivileged caller can
gain the privilege needed to create namespaces and cgroups; it drops
irreversibly to a per-box unprivileged UID before executing the program.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B \-\-init
Create the box directory tree for the given
.B \-\-box\-id
and print the box root path to standard output. Fails if the box is already
initialized.
.TP
.B \-\-run \-\- program [args...]
Run
.I program
inside the initialized box under the configured limits. The program and its
arguments follow a
.B \-\-
separator. Paths are relative to the sandbox root.
.TP
.B \-\-cleanup
Remove the box directory tree and cgroup and clear the box lock. Idempotent.
.TP
.BI \-\-yaml= FILE
Standalone mode: run the tasks described by
.IR FILE .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Identification
.TP
.BI \-\-box\-id= ID
Integer box id (0\(en5000), unique per concurrent invocation. Required for
.BR \-\-init / \-\-run / \-\-cleanup .
The box UID/GID, filesystem root, and cgroup are all derived from it.
.TP
.BI \-\-meta= FILE
Write the isolate-format meta-file (task result and statistics) to
.IR FILE .
.SS Resource limits
.TP
.BI \-\-mem= KB
.TQ
.BI \-\-cg\-mem= KB
Memory limit in kilobytes, enforced via the cgroup.
.TP
.BI \-\-as\-size= KB
Address-space limit in kilobytes
.RB ( RLIMIT_AS ).
.TP
.BI \-\-time= SECONDS
CPU time limit (fractional seconds).
.TP
.BI \-\-wall\-time= SECONDS
Wall-clock time limit (fractional seconds), enforced by the supervisor.
.TP
.BI \-\-extra\-time= SECONDS
Grace period added before a timed-out process is reported.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack= KB
Stack size limit in kilobytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-fsize= KB
Maximum output file size in kilobytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-open\-files= N
Maximum number of open file descriptors.
.TP
.BI \-\-core= KB
Maximum core-dump size in kilobytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-quota= "BLOCKS,INODES"
Disk quota of the box: at most
.I BLOCKS
1\ KiB quota blocks (not filesystem blocks) and
.I INODES
files, kept by the kernel against the box's user, so it counts every file
the box owns.
A 0 leaves that cap unlimited.
Set by
.B \-\-init
(which clears any earlier cap when the flag is absent), changed by a
.B \-\-run
that passes it, and ignored by
.BR \-\-cleanup .
The box tree must be on a filesystem that enforces user quotas (e.g. ext4
mounted
.BR usrquota );
otherwise the phase fails with exit status 2.
.TP
.BR \-\-processes [ =\fIN\fR ]
Maximum number of processes/threads. Bare
.B \-\-processes
means unlimited.
.SS CPU and memory-node pinning
Restrict the whole box (the supervising processes and every task) to a
set of CPU cores and NUMA memory nodes, through the cgroup cpuset controller.
Only
.B \-\-run
is pinned: the box's control group is rebuilt on every run, so pass the flags
on every
.BR \-\-run .
.B \-\-init
and
.B \-\-cleanup
accept and ignore them.
.PP
A
.I LIST
is comma-separated ids or ranges, e.g.
.BR 2 ,
.B 0,2,4
or
.BR 1\-4,6 .
Other forms the kernel accepts (strides,
.BR N ,
.BR all )
and an empty list are refused (exit status 2).
.PP
Pinning is exact: if the kernel rejects the list (no such CPU or node), or
grants less than all of it (a requested CPU or node is offline), the run fails
with exit status 2 and
.B status:XX
rather than proceeding on a different set.
The Guardian does not check boxes against each other; overlapping sets are the
caller's choice.
Pin with these flags, not with
.BR taskset (1)
around the Guardian: on kernels 6.2 and later the two masks intersect, and on
older ones the
.BR taskset (1)
mask is lost.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpuset\-cpus= LIST
CPU cores the box may run on (written to
.IR cpuset.cpus ).
.TP
.BI \-\-cpuset\-mems= LIST
NUMA memory nodes the box may allocate memory from (written to
.IR cpuset.mems ).
This does not move the box onto the nodes' CPUs; combine it with
.B \-\-cpuset\-cpus
for that.
.SS Input/output
.TP
.BI \-\-stdin= FILE
Redirect standard input from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-stdout= FILE
Redirect standard output to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-stderr= FILE
Redirect standard error to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-stderr\-to\-stdout
Send standard error to the same destination as standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-chdir= DIR
Change to
.I DIR
(relative to the sandbox root) before running the program.
.SS Environment and filesystem
.TP
.BI \-\-env= RULE
Environment variable rule:
.BI VAR = VALUE
sets a variable,
.I VAR
inherits it from the caller. May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir= RULE
Directory rule mapping a host path into the sandbox, e.g.
.IB in = out
or
.IB in = out : OPTIONS
(options include
.BR rw ,
.BR maybe ,
.BR fs ,
.BR tmp ,
.BR dev ,
.BR noexec ,
.BR norec ).
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.B \-\-share\-net
Do not create a network namespace; the sandbox shares the host network.
.SS Miscellaneous
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Verbose diagnostic logging to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-\-cg ", " \-\-cg\-timing
Accepted for isolate compatibility and ignored (control groups are always used).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran and exited with status 0.
.TP
.B 1
The program ran but the result was not OK (non-zero exit, killed by a signal,
or a limit was exceeded); details are in the meta-file.
.TP
.B 2
The Guardian itself failed (bad arguments, setup error, or the box was not
initialized).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/recodex-guardian/boxes/<box-id>
The per-box directory tree (the writable
.I box/
working directory lives here).
.TP
.I /run/recodex-guardian/locks/<box-id>
Per-box lock and initialization record (tmpfs).
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/recodex-guardian
The cgroup subtree for all boxes; created and given controllers on first
.BR \-\-run .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR isolate (1)
(the upstream IOI sandbox this tool replaces).
